=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: false
faulty_devices: none
diagnosis_text:
The reported symptom — that the default gateway (192.168.200.1) "drops out for a
few seconds at random" for Location C users (PC11-PC14, 192.168.200.0/24) — does
not correspond to any real fault in the current lab state. Direct measurement shows
that all four PCs can continuously reach router4's LAN interface (eth0,
192.168.200.1/24) with zero packet loss and sub-millisecond latency. Router4's eth0
has recorded zero link-up and zero link-down events since the container started,
confirming that no interface flap has occurred. The FRR RIP database contains no
competing advertisement for 192.168.200.0/24 from any peer, so there is no route
oscillation that could temporarily displace the connected route. The L2 forwarding
path through switch4, switch5, and switch6 is fully stable — all bridge ports are in
STP forwarding state with no topology-change timer activity. There are no tc/netem
traffic-shaping rules injected on router4's LAN interface, no crontab entries that
could periodically disrupt it, and the startup configuration is clean with no injected
faults. The root cause is that the helpdesk complaint describes an intermittent
condition that does not exist in this network: the gateway is stable and fully
reachable from all Location C hosts. No corrective action is required; the ticket
should be closed as a false alarm, and if users continue to observe issues, the
problem should be investigated at the client-side (e.g., ARP cache behavior, local
firewall, or NIC driver) rather than at the router or switch layer.
=== END ===
```